INT. GREYHOUND DEPOT - WAITING ROOM - 2 A.M.

Molded plastic seats. A flickering departures board. A janitor's cart abandoned beside a drinking fountain. Four people share one row, each at a careful distance.

CORMAC BEAUFORT (40s, trench coat, newspaper folded to the crossword) taps a pen against his knee. ODALIE MWANGI-REID (30s, parka, a duffel between her feet) watches the doors. VINCENZO PALLADINO (50s, windbreaker, fingers laced over a paunch) stares at the ceiling. TALLULAH BRIGHT (20s, headphones, backpack on her lap) bites her thumbnail.

                    CORMAC
          Nine letters. "Careless about the future."

                    ODALIE
          Improvident.

                    CORMAC
          That's ten.

                    ODALIE
          Then I don't know.

                    VINCENZO
          Reckless.

                    CORMAC
          Eight.

                    VINCENZO
          Then it's something else.

                    TALLULAH
          (muttering)
          Everything's something else.

                    CORMAC
          Pardon?

                    TALLULAH
          Nothing. Talking to myself.

                    ODALIE
          Who's your bus?

                    TALLULAH
          Three-forty to Toledo.

                    ODALIE
          Same.

                    TALLULAH
          Really?

                    ODALIE
          Don't sound so thrilled.

                    CORMAC
          That makes three of us.

                    VINCENZO
          Four.

                    CORMAC
          Of course.

                    ODALIE
          Funny. Four strangers, same bus, same hour, none of us sleeping.

                    VINCENZO
          People don't sleep before a journey.

                    ODALIE
          Or after a crime.

Silence. The board flickers. A vending machine hums.

                    TALLULAH
          That's dark.

                    ODALIE
          That's the news.

                    CORMAC
          I read that a jewelry store on Fifth was hit last night. Eleven grand in diamonds.

                    VINCENZO
          Eleven? I read forty.

                    CORMAC
          Papers exaggerate.

                    VINCENZO
          Or they underplay.

                    ODALIE
          Which paper?

                    CORMAC
          The Courier.

                    ODALIE
          The Courier says seventeen.

                    CORMAC
          You read it?

                    ODALIE
          I read everything. It's a habit.

                    TALLULAH
          Can I ask something?

                    ODALIE
          Go on.

                    TALLULAH
          Why does everyone here have a bag bigger than they need?

Nobody answers. Cormac refolds his paper. Odalie nudges her duffel with her toe. Vincenzo unlaces his fingers and rests his hands, palms up, on his knees.

                    VINCENZO
          Because travel is unpredictable.

                    TALLULAH
          And your bag is clanking.

                    VINCENZO
          Cutlery.

                    TALLULAH
          Sure.

                    CORMAC
          (to the room, quietly)
          Five minutes to boarding. Let's all be sensible.

                    ODALIE
          Sensible is my middle name.

                    TALLULAH
          Mine's Marguerite.

                    VINCENZO
          Mine's Giuseppe.

                    CORMAC
          Mine's classified.

They look at one another and, one by one, begin to laugh, an exhausted, hollow, shared laugh that rolls down the row.
